    "body": "<!-- claude-projects-attribution: {\"github_login\":\"siainbuletin\"} -->\n_Requested by **Sia** · [project thread](https://claude.ai/code/project/chan_01KaQfLZaF43QUMN3kcT7pW2?thread=cmsg_01KaQfLZaF43QUMN3kcT7pW23eddj9am1nebvkG14YLgqW)_\n\n#85 started `cursor-intangible-mode` from an idle timer, but the echo-area error still appeared constantly. This removes the mode.\n\n**Why the error cannot be suppressed**\n- The mode puts `cursor-sensor--move-to-tangible` on `pre-redisplay-functions`. It calls `cursor-sensor-tangible-pos`, which returns nil when it finds no tangible position, then does arithmetic on nil: `(wrong-type-argument number-or-marker-p nil)` (Sia's backtrace).\n- Redisplay runs the hook inside `with-demoted-errors`, so the error is caught and printed in C-level redisplay before any handler of ours runs. `condition-case`, `debug-on-error` and `warning-suppress-types` cannot reach it. Advising the function would mean patching Emacs's own code.\n- It repeats because redisplay runs the hook every time, for as long as the condition holds.\n- Edge cases where the mode would work: a buffer that is never erased, never redrawn and always has a button. The dashboard redraws on every window-state change (`sp-dashboard-refresh`), and the buttons come and go with the privacy mode and agenda boxes, so it cannot meet that. A guard that makes it safe would have to run before redisplay, which is the same hook.\n\n**Changes** (`sp-dashboard.el`, `sp-dashboard.org`, `CHANGELOG.org`)\n- `sp-dashboard--isolate-menu` no longer marks text `cursor-intangible` or starts the mode; `sp-dashboard--enable-intangible` is removed. The keymap overlay stays: `TAB` / `S-TAB` already step through the buttons (wrapping), and the commands that move point are bound to `ignore`, so point only rests on a button.\n- `sp-dashboard-focus-face` has `:extend nil`.\n- `global-hl-line-mode` is turned off in the dashboard buffer: `(setq-local hl-line-mode -1)` does not disable it, and `prog/sp-prog-misc.el` turns the global mode on. This is the likely cause of the whole-line highlight, since the focus overlay covers only the button text; not verified.\n\n**Not tested:** no Emacs in this environment, so none of it was run.\n\n---\n_Generated by [Claude Code](https://claude.ai/code/session_01U17WoSwrRGjp7q9svv9TBQ)_",
